Chapter 14
Chapter 13 - Volatility: Range, ATR
and Why Stops Need Context
14.1 Learning objectives
You should understand realized price variability, True Range and ATR, volatility-normalized thinking,
why fixed pip stops are inconsistent across regimes, and why higher volatility requires lower position size
for the same risk budget.
14.2 What volatility means
Volatility describes the magnitude/variability of price movement, not direction. A market can be highly
volatile while ending unchanged after violent swings.
For a trader, volatility influences:
• reasonable stop distances;
• position size;
• expected trade duration;
• spread/slippage behaviour;
• breakout frequency;
• risk of gaps and fast moves.
14.3 True Range
A simple high-low range ignores gaps between bars. True Range commonly uses the largest of:
1. current high - current low;
2. absolute(current high - previous close);
3. absolute(current low - previous close).
