A rulebook must state the timeframe stack before the trade.
18.5 Conflicting timeframes
Conflicts are normal. You do not need to force alignment. Your strategy may:
• trade only when all required timeframes agree;
• trade countertrend setups under specific conditions;
• stay flat when context is mixed.
Staying flat is a legitimate trading decision.
18.6 The 100-chart gate
Before progressing to macro analysis, complete 100 annotated historical charts . Each chart should
contain:
• symbol and date;
• timeframe;
• trend/range/unclear label;
• major swings;
• 2-4 significant support/resistance zones;
• volatility note;
• possible invalidation level;
• higher-timeframe context;
• what you believe would matter next;
• later outcome recorded separately.
Do not score yourself on whether you predicted the future. Score yourself on whether your description
was consistent, factual and reproducible.
18.7 Technical-literacy gate
You should now be able to:
• explain candles mathematically;
• identify swings;
• describe trend/range without hindsight;
• explain support/resistance as probabilistic areas;
• define breakouts objectively;
• calculate/interpret ATR;
• explain moving averages and oscillators from their inputs;
• recognize common patterns without treating them as certainty;
• perform top-down analysis with a fixed timeframe stack.
