Chapter 42
Chapter 41 - News, Weekend, Gap and
Tail-Risk Controls
42.1 Learning objectives
You should understand ordinary versus tail risk, define event restrictions before entry, and build kill-switch
rules that prevent one unusual period from becoming an uncontrolled account event.
42.2 Ordinary risk versus discontinuity
Your backtest may assume continuous bar-to-bar prices. Real markets can gap. During an unexpected
shock, the next tradable price may be beyond your stop. This creates gap/slippage risk .
42.3 Scheduled high-impact risk
Examples include:
• central-bank rate decisions;
• CPI/inflation;
• payroll/employment releases;
• major fiscal announcements;
• elections/referendums;
• major speeches.
A strategy can either:
1. deliberately trade the event under tested rules;
2. remain flat/avoid new entries within a defined window;
3. hold through events because historical evidence supports it.
What is prohibited is improvising differently every time.
