Chapter 54
Chapter 53 - Data Integrity, Time,
Sessions and Look-Ahead Bias
54.1 Learning objectives
You should understand why timestamp handling can invalidate a test, detect missing/duplicate bars
conceptually, and avoid using information before it existed.
54.2 Completed-bar principle
If a rule uses the Daily close, the signal is not known until that Daily bar is complete according to the
data feed’s session convention. Entering at a price from earlier that day is look-ahead bias.
54.3 Common look-ahead mistakes
• using the day’s high to enter earlier in the same day;
• using a revised economic figure as if the revision were known originally;
• using the final shape of an H4 candle before it closed;
• placing support/resistance using future swing points;
• selecting only instruments that survived or remained liquid through the sample.
54.4 Time zones
Broker H4/D1 candles can differ depending on server timezone and daily close convention. Record:
• data source;
• timezone;
• daylight-saving treatment;
• session boundaries.
A strategy based on H4 bars may produce different signals on different feeds.
