Chapter 63 - The Trading Journal as an
Evidence Database
64.1 Learning objectives
You should build a journal that records what was known before the trade, distinguishes process from
outcome and produces useful review metrics.
64.2 The journal is not a diary of feelings alone
A good journal has three layers:
1. decision data - what you knew/planned before entry;
2. execution data - what actually happened at entry/exit;
3. review data - rule adherence, emotion, MAE/MFE, lesson.
64.3 Core fields
The reference framework recommends:
• trade ID;
• date/time and broker/local reference;
• pair;
• strategy/version;
• direction;
• market regime;
• entry reason;
• entry;
• initial stop and distance;
• planned exit;
• equity;
• risk % and cash risk;
• position size;
