Chapter 76
Chapter 75 - Funded/Prop Trading
Programs: Evaluate Later, Not as a
Shortcut
76.1 Learning objectives
You should understand that a challenge program changes the rule environment and counterparty relation-
ship, and why it should only be considered after you have an independently validated process.
76.2 The attraction
Prop/funded programs often market access to larger notional capital in exchange for evaluation fees and
strict rules. The appeal is obvious for traders with small personal capital.
76.3 The problems to analyze
Before considering any program, examine:
• legal entity and jurisdiction;
• whether trading is simulated or live;
• fee structure;
• payout terms;
• maximum daily/overall drawdown calculation;
• news/weekend restrictions;
• consistency rules;
• prohibited strategies;
• account termination clauses;
• actual history of payouts and complaints;
• whether the business model aligns with your trading style.
Rules can create a different strategy problem. For example, a valid low-frequency trend system may
violate a tight trailing drawdown rule through ordinary variance.
